    "q-79809": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79809",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+0\\cdot (1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
