    "q-86715": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86715",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{3}-y),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12{z}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,-3,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,-3,1) & =(2\\cdot 2)\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y+3\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,-3+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx-\\,Δy+3\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-2-(-3)=1 \\\\ Δz & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\,Δx-1\\,Δy+3\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
