    "q-79810": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79810",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
