    "q-79838": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79838",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Estimate $\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\sqrt{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x-2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx-2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5)=f(3.9,1.5),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3.9,1.5).$\n\nMoving from $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(3.9,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3.9-4=-0.1, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈-0.070796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.1)-2(-0.070796) \\\\  & ≈0+0.141592 \\\\  & ≈0.141592.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141592.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9,1.5) & ≈f(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})+0.141592 \\\\ \\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5) & ≈\\sqrt{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+0.141592 \\\\  & ≈0+0.141592 \\\\  & ≈0.142,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
