    "q-86717": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86717",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Estimate ${e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ at the point $(0,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,2),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =2\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}x+0\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,2+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈2\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}=f(0.3,2.15),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.3,2.15).$\n\nMoving from $(0,2)$ to $(0.3,2.15),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.3-0=0.3, \\\\ Δy & =2.15-2=0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈2\\cdot (0.3)+0\\cdot (0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.6.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.3,2.15) & ≈f(0,2)+0.6 \\\\ {e}^{(0.3)(2.15)} & ≈{e}^{(0)(2)}+0.6 \\\\  & ≈1+0.6 \\\\  & ≈1.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
