    "q-37281": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37281",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Estimate $\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+\\sqrt{y}$ at the point $(8,144).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(8,144),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {8}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{144}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(8+Δx,144+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{12}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{24}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}=f(10,150),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,150).$\n\nMoving from $(8,144)$ to $(10,150),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-8=2, \\\\ Δy & =150-144=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{12}(2)+\\frac{1}{24}(6) \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & ≈0.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.417.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,150) & ≈f(8,144)+0.417 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150} & ≈\\sqrt[3]{8}+\\sqrt{144}+0.417 \\\\  & ≈2+12+0.417 \\\\  & ≈14.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
